{"kind":"report","text":"Milestone A advice (intermediate, not the implementation verdict). Inspected branch request/initial-tailapp-product; go test and go vet pass on the milestone code.\n\nPROVENANCE CORRECTION FIRST: the request and the retired artifacts cite head 699c206ac35a8f0be73b2f7e073f5f64e5c65a2f, which does not exist in this repository. The real milestone commit is 699c2064ea64316688e02e26bed434304081ced8 (the branch tip; only the short prefix 699c206 matches). This advice covers that real commit. The full hash appears to have been hand-expanded; always cite git rev-parse output verbatim, because final approval binds to an exact head and a fabricated hash would poison that provenance.\n\nOVERALL: milestone A is strong work. The storage-shape compatibility rule (normalized token stream retaining constraints, ContinueCompatible allowing additive tables), revision/export digest identity (RuntimeID inside the source digest, contract digests over normalized export schemas), single-writer topology with transitive view resolution and cycle detection, and the evaluator output validation (unknown-field rejection, ineffective purity, exact-integer range, per-operation row completeness) all faithfully implement the approved design. The bundled agent-guard honors the honest-coverage contract: unknown coverage yields insufficient-telemetry findings and never a compliance claim, and denied-tool findings still fire with coverage_state unknown when target detail is gated.\n\nBLOCKING CORRECTION B1 (empirically confirmed): comma-join views evade read ownership. queryRelations extracts only the first relation from \"FROM a, b\", and validateQueryShape does not refuse comma joins, so CREATE VIEW leak AS SELECT v, w FROM mine, other compiles, and an analytic fold READ over that view reads a table owned by another analytic fold. I verified this with a probe: the profile compiles where it must be refused. Fix before milestone B: either parse comma-separated FROM lists into dependencies or refuse the comma form in views, exports, and reads; add the refusal to the milestone A test set alongside TestReadAuthorityTraversesViews.\n\nBLOCKING DETERMINATION B2 — the JSONata question you asked: the static nonrecursive allowlisted subset plus input/depth/range/output bounds is NOT sufficient for the deterministic production profile, for three confirmed reasons. (1) Object iteration is nondeterministic: the pinned jsonata-go port's own DIFFERENCES.md states Go map iteration is explicitly randomized, and wildcard paths (* and **) are syntax, not $-functions, so the allowlist does not exclude them — identical revision and input can produce differently ordered sequences. (2) There is no step bound: the library offers only SetMaxDepth, SetMaxRange, and wall-clock SetMaxTime; nested generative operators multiply beyond any single bound (for example [1..4096].[1..4096] is a ~16.7M-element intermediate, each range individually legal), and a wall-clock abort is machine-dependent, so the same program can gap on one machine and commit on another. (3) There is no allocation bound: intermediate sequences and concatenated strings can exceed the output limit by orders of magnitude before the post-evaluation size check, and the engine process hosts every tailapp, so one hostile program is an engine-wide OOM, violating isolation. Smallest sufficient instrumentation: a narrow fork of the v206 evaluator that (a) adds an evaluation step counter in the main dispatch mirroring the existing depthCounter plumbing, with a deterministic budget error; (b) adds a cumulative sequence-element/string-byte allocation budget checked at construction sites; (c) sorts keys at every observable map-to-sequence site (the port already sorts in at least one path). Fold both budgets into RuntimeID. Cheap interim until the fork lands, viable for milestone B: statically reject * and ** wildcards in JSONata source (the bundled apps use only $lookup and lose nothing), and keep SetMaxTime strictly as an outer safety net — hoisted from the hardcoded 250 to a named profile constant — never the primary bound for active revisions.\n\nADVISORIES, non-blocking: (1) CREATE TABLE statements skip the ambient-function check; SQLite happens to reject nondeterministic functions in CHECK constraints, but add the explicit compiler refusal for defense in depth and better diagnostics. (2) Word-boundary scans (forbiddenSQLRE, functionCallRE) hit string literals — CHECK (kind IN ('update')) would be falsely refused; acceptable conservatism now, worth tokenizing later. (3) validateTopology admits BOOLEAN and JSON event columns as read parameters; define BOOLEAN binding (0/1) and exclude JSON from parameters, matching the spec's scalar-fields wording. (4) At default content gates, target detail is usually absent, so guard.jsonata emits one insufficient-telemetry policy finding per tool event — a per-event firehose that will dominate the findings export; consider recording insufficient-telemetry once per harness/session/capability and leaving per-event coverage to telemetry_coverage. (5) telemetry_coverage declares state 'missing' but the normalizer can never produce it; implement or drop. (6) loop_findings finding_id omits source_position so each kind/session keeps only the latest row while policy findings accumulate per position — if intentional, document the distinction. Proceed to milestone B with B1 fixed and the B2 path chosen (interim wildcard rejection plus planned fork, or fork first)."}